<body>
<h1>Unit 11: Quantity of Heat</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Heat &amp; Thermodynamics</p><p class="summary">Quantity of heat is where the first law becomes arithmetic: energy accounting with a heat capacity, a latent heat and a clear statement of which system you are describing.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Q = m c dT requires no phase change, a constant specific heat over the range, and a clearly defined system.</li><li>Specific heat capacity is per unit mass; the molar heat capacity is C = M c, where M is the molar mass.</li><li>During a phase change the temperature stays constant while heat is added, because the energy goes into changing the structure rather than the motion of the particles.</li><li>Latent heat enters as Q = m L; the specific latent heat of vaporisation of water at 100 degrees C is about 2.26e6 J/kg.</li><li>Internal energy is a state function of the body; heat is the transfer, and the first law writes dQ = dU + dW with work done by the system positive.</li><li>Calorimetry rests on the statement that heat lost by the hot body equals heat gained by the cold one - true only for an isolated system.</li><li>Heat capacity is quoted along constant volume or constant pressure because only the path-independent change of internal energy is the same for both; the difference between them is the work done by expansion.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The constant-c approximation fails for gases, whose heat capacity varies with temperature, and for any interval that crosses a phase change.</li><li>Latent heat is not a universal constant: it varies with pressure and temperature, and for water it falls as the critical point is approached.</li><li>Calorimetry assumes an isolated system; in a real cup the heat capacity of the container must be included, and it often dominates the error.</li><li>Specific heat of water is commonly approximated as 4.18 kJ/(kg.K) over 0 to 100 degrees C, but it varies by more than 1 % across that range.</li><li>Water's large specific heat is a symptom of hydrogen-bonded network rearrangements, not a separate slow-to-heat mechanism, and it is the reason oceans moderate coastal climate.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 6 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Newton's law of cooling</li><li>Measurement of specific heat capacity of solids and liquids</li><li>Change of phases: latent heat</li><li>Specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization</li><li>Measurement of specific latent heat of fusion and vaporization</li><li>Triple point</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“While water boils, the temperature of the water keeps rising because the flame is still heating it.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> During a phase change the energy supplied goes into breaking intermolecular bonds (latent heat), not into raising the kinetic energy of the molecules. The temperature stays at the boiling point until the liquid has all become vapour.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Q = m L is the energy needed for the change of phase at constant temperature. For water at 100 degrees C, L = 2.26 x 10^6 J/kg; boiling 1 kg absorbs 2.26 MJ with no change in temperature.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The specific heat capacity of water is the heat needed to raise 1 kg of water by 1 degree C, so it is the same for all bodies with the same mass.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Specific heat capacity is a property of the material, not of the mass alone. Water's is unusually large because of the hydrogen-bonded structure, and the value also depends slightly on the temperature range and on whether the heating is at constant pressure or constant volume.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> c is characteristic of the material: about 4200 J/(kg K) for water, 900 for aluminium, 380 for copper, 840 for glass. The heat needed is Q = m c dT, so it depends on the mass, the material and the temperature change together.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A cold bottle taken out of a refrigerator warms up at a steady rate until it reaches room temperature.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> The rate of warming falls as the temperature difference shrinks, following Newton's law of cooling. The curve is exponential, never a straight line, and in practice the last few degrees take a long time.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> dT/dt = -k (T - T_s), so T - T_s = (T_0 - T_s) e^(-k t). The rate of change is largest at the start and tends to zero as equilibrium is approached.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>How much heat is needed to melt 0.50 kg of ice at 0 degrees C and then raise the resulting water to 20 degrees C? Take L_f = 3.34 x 10^5 J/kg and c_water = 4200 J/(kg K).</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Melting: Q1 = m L = 0.50 x 3.34 x 10^5 = 1.67 x 10^5 J (at constant temperature). Warming: Q2 = m c dT = 0.50 x 4200 x 20 = 4.2 x 10^4 J. Total Q = 1.67 x 10^5 + 4.2 x 10^4 = 2.09 x 10^5 J. Note that the first stage changes no temperature at all, which is the point of the latent heat.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive Newton's law of cooling from the assumption that the rate of heat loss is proportional to the excess of temperature over the surroundings.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Let the body's temperature be T and the surroundings T_s, and let the heat lost per second be proportional to the excess: dQ/dt = -k (T - T_s), with k &gt; 0. By the definition of heat, dQ = m c dT for a body of constant specific heat, so m c dT/dt = -k (T - T_s). Separating variables, dT/(T - T_s) = -(k/m c) dt. Integrating from T_0 at t = 0 gives ln[(T - T_s)/(T_0 - T_s)] = -(k/m c) t, so T - T_s = (T_0 - T_s) e^(-k t / m c). The excess temperature therefore falls exponentially, with time constant m c / k.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>A kettle of water is heated on a stove. The water reaches a boil and then the temperature stops rising. Explain the energy balance once boiling starts, and identify where the energy is going.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Once boiling starts, the temperature stays at the boiling point (100 degrees C at 1 atm) because the energy supplied is used as latent heat of vaporisation: the rate of vaporisation is set by the rate of energy supply, Q/t = m_dot L. The energy goes into overcoming the intermolecular attraction that holds the liquid together, and the escaping vapour carries away the energy that a thermometer would otherwise register. Some is also lost to the vessel and the air. This is why boiling is a good way to cook: the temperature is capped at 100 degrees C, and the energy delivered goes into turning liquid into steam rather than into overheating the water.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Specific heat of water</td><td>about 4.18 kJ/(kg.K)</td></tr><tr><td>Latent heat of vaporisation of water (100 degrees C)</td><td>about 2.26e6 J/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Specific latent heat of fusion of water at 0 degrees C</td><td>about 3.34e5 J/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Energy to raise 1 kg of water by 1 K</td><td>about 4.18 kJ</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>How is energy distributed among the translational, rotational and vibrational modes of a molecule as its temperature rises?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Equipartition gives the high-temperature limit and quantum theory gives the low-temperature limit; the crossover in real materials is modelled, not derived.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can the heat capacity of a complex solid be computed from first principles to experimental accuracy?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Modern ab initio methods approximate it well for simple crystals; no exact general theory exists.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Plan a calorimetry experiment to determine the specific heat of a metal and list every systematic error you can foresee.</li><li>Show quantitatively why 1 kg of steam at 100 degrees C can release more energy than 1 kg of water at 100 degrees C.</li><li>Estimate the energy needed to heat a classroom by 10 degrees C and compare it with a student's daily food intake.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/quantity-of-heat">Read the chapter notes: Quantity of Heat</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-calc-heat">Heat calculator</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-3d-heat-determinations">Heat determinations 3D lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://physics.nist.gov/cuu/Uncertainty/basic.html">NIST: Measurement uncertainty</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-4-heat-transfer-specific-heat-and-calorimetry">OpenStax: Heat Transfer, Specific Heat and Calorimetry</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/1-5-phase-changes">OpenStax: Phase Changes</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
</body>
